prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D rope physics demonstration based on Verlet integration and distance constraints (do not use Ammo.js; this task retains the directory name P22_ammo_rope).

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Place the following import map in <head> (include it verbatim), and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:

<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>

<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Rope Model]
(1) Use a total of 30 particle segments (segmentCount=30), with adjacent segments connected by distance constraints;
(2) Integration: Verlet; solve the distance constraints iteratively through relaxation each frame, with constraintIterations >= 5;
(3) Gravitational acceleration g=9.81, in the direction (0,-1,0) (world units only need to be consistent with the display);
(4) The top particle is fixed at the ceiling position topPin, with coordinates approximately (0, 5, 0) (very small numerical errors are allowed); the bottom end is free;
(5) Stiffness slider: id="stiffness", type="range", with a value range of 0.5–1.0, representing the constraint relaxation coefficient or stiffness weight (1.0 is the stiffest); the implementation must actually use this value in the simulation to affect the degree of constraint satisfaction;
(6) Wind toggle: id="windToggle" (a button or checkbox may be used); when enabled, apply a horizontal wind force to the rope particles (such as +X or a world-space horizontal vector); when disabled, apply no wind force;

[Interaction]
(7) Use mouse clicking to pick and grab a particle/segment on the rope. While dragging, constrain that point to the intersection of the mouse ray with a plane (or use an equivalent 3D dragging method); cancel the grab when the mouse button is released;
(8) Use OrbitControls to rotate the view, avoiding conflicts with the dragging logic (for example, by distinguishing mouse buttons or modifier keys);

[Rendering]
(9) Represent each particle with a small SphereGeometry sphere; connect adjacent particles using CylinderGeometry (or thin cylinders) to create the appearance of a continuous rope;
(10) Provide simple lighting and a ground/sky or reference grid to make observation easier;

[State Exposure (Required, Updated Every Frame)]
window.__3D_STATE__ must contain:
  - segmentCount: number (30)
  - topPin: { x, y, z }
  - bottomPos: { x, y, z } (world coordinates of the bottommost particle)
  - avgStretch: number, defined as the average of "current length / rest length" across all adjacent segments (use a single restLength when all rest lengths are identical)
  - windEnabled: boolean
  - stiffness: number (matching the current value of #stiffness)
  - simTime: number (accumulated simulation time, in seconds)

[Numerical Stability]
(11) NaN/Infinity must not occur during long-running execution; particle coordinates must remain finite.

Output a single HTML file that can run by being opened directly in a browser.
